  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Creolimax fragrantissima genome dataset shows early but real scholarly uptake: it has one citation in OpenAlex and three documented downstream reuse records. The record is still sparse, but it already appears in the reuse and citation trail of later studies on closest living relatives of animals.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in Figshare in 2015 by Alex De Mendoza, Hiroshi Suga, and I\u00f1aki Ruiz-Trillo, this dataset provides the genome assembly of the ichthyosporean Creolimax fragrantissima, together with annotation, mRNA, and protein sequences supporting the associated research article. Its subject profile centers on genome science, computational biology, genetics, and gene-level analysis, which fits its role as a reusable reference resource rather than a standalone interpretive paper.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The impact record points to focused scholarly reuse rather than broad public attention. OpenAlex shows one citation, and the reuse graph captures three downstream reuse signals: one article-level reuse and two biomedical article mentions in Europe PMC. The citing and reusing literature is concentrated in evolutionary and protist-focused research, including a 2025 eLife article on G-protein-coupled receptor diversity and evolution in the closest living relatives of metazoa.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This pattern suggests that the dataset is being used as enabling infrastructure for comparative genomics and evolutionary biology. The downstream work spans topics such as protist diversity and phylogeny and marine and coastal ecosystems, indicating that the data have value beyond the original genome announcement and into later analyses of metazoan relatives.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Stewardship signals are also strong for access and persistence: the dataset is openly available through Figshare, and repository indexing shows open instances of the record. At the same time, the broader attention record is quiet, with no captured online mentions and no available public-discourse archive in the sources provided. Overall, the evidence supports a concise narrative of a well-stewarded dataset with modest but clear scholarly reuse.</p>",
